RANKING: A > D > F > C > B > E

A is most clearly written for an adult. Its signals are social taboo, old age, work, communal silence, ambiguous grief, and dialogue whose evasions matter more than its literal information. E is most clearly not: it addresses “amigo lector,” asks the reader to count, supplies praise, uses sound effects, and closes with an elementary moral.

A is the strongest because it makes one physical object—the untouched table—carry atmosphere, plot, communal history, and emotional absence simultaneously. It delivers a complete dramatic encounter while leaving a larger mystery open.

E is the weakest because it infantilizes the buyer directly. Its coherent plot and tidy closure cannot compensate for asking an adult app graduate whether they can count eight arms.

Yes. I would pay for A and finish a book that sustained its control of mystery and concrete detail. D, F, and C could support that same book, though their unnatural constructions need editing. B and E are not pleasure reading for this audience.

